      "p1_hook": "The relationship between religion and the secular is not a zero-sum teleology of disenchantment, but a complex, historically contingent reconfiguration of authority, hermeneutics, and public space. Graduating with a 3.7/4.0 GPA in Islamic Studies and an Associate Degree in Politics, Philosophy, and Economics (PPE), my scholarship has focused on how religious traditions respond to modern institutional structures. Supported by literacy in classical and modern Arabic and Persian, I have investigated how Islamic jurisprudence and political theology adapted to colonial and post-colonial secular legal frameworks. The M.A. in Religion and the Secular at Universität Leipzig offers the premier international crucible to deepen this inquiry.",
